{
  "id": "time-based-key-value-store",
  "questionId": "time-based-key-value-store",
  "slug": "time-based-key-value-store",
  "title": "Time Based Key-Value Store",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Binary search",
  "patterns": [
    "binary search",
    "hash map",
    "design"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "grind-75"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "since timestamps for a given key only ever increase across set calls, each key's history is naturally already sorted by timestamp - no separate sort step needed",
    "a get is asking: among this key's recorded timestamps, find the largest one that is <= the query timestamp",
    "'largest value at or below a target, in a sorted list' is exactly what binary search is built for",
    "binary search for the rightmost timestamp <= the query; if none exists (the query is smaller than every recorded timestamp for that key), return -1",
    "keep a hash map of key -> list of (timestamp, value) pairs appended in order as sets arrive, and binary search that list on each get"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "scanning linearly for the right timestamp instead of binary searching, which is correct but misses the intended O(log n) approach",
    "returning the closest timestamp instead of specifically the largest one that does not exceed the query",
    "forgetting to return -1 when the query timestamp is smaller than every timestamp ever set for that key",
    "conflating two different keys' histories instead of keeping a separate, independently sorted list per key"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a space-delimited sequence of operations `ops` against a time-based key-value store, where keys and values are small integers. Each token is one of:\n\n- `set_<key>_<value>_<timestamp>` - store value for key at the given timestamp, no return value\n- `get_<key>_<timestamp>` - return the value that was set for key at the largest recorded timestamp less than or equal to the given timestamp, or -1 if no such value exists\n\n**Simulate the operations in order on an initially empty store, and return a list containing the result of every \"get\" operation, in the order they occurred.**\n\nFor any single key, every set call for that key arrives with a strictly increasing timestamp.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= number of operations <= 2 * 10^5",
      "0 <= key, value <= 10^6",
      "1 <= timestamp <= 10^7",
      "for any single key, all set calls for that key use strictly increasing timestamps"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "get called on a key that was never set (always -1)",
      "get with a timestamp earlier than every set recorded for that key (-1)",
      "get with a timestamp exactly matching a recorded set timestamp",
      "get with a timestamp far later than the latest set for that key (returns the latest value)"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "ops = \"set_1_100_1 get_1_1 get_1_3 set_1_200_4 get_1_4 get_1_5\"",
        "output": "[100, 100, 200, 200]",
        "expectedOutput": "[100, 100, 200, 200]",
        "explanation": "at timestamp 3, the latest set for key 1 at or before it is still timestamp 1 (value 100); at timestamp 4 and 5, it's timestamp 4 (value 200)"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "ops = \"set_2_50_5 get_2_1 get_2_5 get_2_10\"",
        "output": "[-1, 50, 50]",
        "expectedOutput": "[-1, 50, 50]",
        "explanation": "get_2_1 queries before key 2's only set (timestamp 5), so it returns -1"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "ops = \"set_3_9_2 set_3_8_4 set_3_7_6 get_3_3 get_3_5 get_3_7\"",
        "output": "[9, 8, 7]",
        "expectedOutput": "[9, 8, 7]",
        "explanation": "each get lands strictly between two set timestamps and returns the value from the most recent one at or before the query"
      }
    ],
